In recent times there has been a significant rise in the relaxation methodologies of Hindu culture. Although for most people in the western part of the world, meditation, yoga or mindfulness is something relatively new, the reality is that These are techniques that Buddhists have been doing for more than 2500 years.
Mindfulness can be defined as the act of focusing attention on the present, attending to the here and now, focusing on what is happening in the moment, accepting it as it is, without judging it and without intention to change it. That is, concentrating on yourself and what happens around you, learning to avoid noise or any of the many existing distractions.
In fact, mindfulness has been used for many years in patients with chronic diseases, since it has been shown to be effective in the treatment of chronic pain. It is also used in many educational centers, to work concentration and improve anxiety in students.
If you want to delve into the ancient art of mindfulness, it is essential that you first discover everything that this Buddhist technique entails. Because as you have already seen, it is not about meditating without more, but it is a compendium of all related techniques, such as yoga, breathing mindfulness or meditation itself.
Knowing oneself in an essential, realistic way, accepting each of the cells that make up one's own being, is the first step to recognize the exceptionality of each individual being. Because before being able to work on you, in those aspects that you would like to improve, it is necessary to accept what you already are, love yourself and respect yourself without punishing yourself for what you are not or for what you cannot do.
