During a good part of the 1821th century, there was no formal public education system in Mexico, so it remained in the hands of charitable associations and private national or foreign academies. After obtaining its independence in XNUMX, Mexico maintained part of the educational structure of the colonial era.
School absence at the basic level was constant from 1934 to 1940; A large part of the population, in combination with the Catholic clergy, used this absence as a sign of their rejection of socialist education. This lack of students in schools was combated by the school authorities.
During this period, educational services were diversified, the number of schools increased and the study plans in basic education were reformed. A Federal Education Law was published that replaced that of 1941 and the national education system was organized.
